Original-layout hardware results
--------------------------------

An original-layout 108-task prefix on queue 1 timed out with `-110`.
An original-layout one-task prefix produced the same result.

A diagnostic KMD recorded the one-task timeout state:

  status=0x0 committed=0x400000
  errors=0x22222222/0x22222222/0x2222
  tq1 status=0x1 vacant=0x400 info=0x0 size=0x9c0000 nid=0x4001

The task manager reported no hardware error. It remained non-idle after the
one-second KMD poll.

DART fault isolation
--------------------

The bring-up self-test left one stale `NO TTBR` fault in DART 1. The normal
DART error acknowledgement cleared its active flag before a clean Qwen
one-task submit.

No DART active-error flag appeared after the Qwen timeout. This rules out an
unmapped buffer as the cause of that clean one-task failure.

Full-graph output inspection
----------------------------

A temporary diagnostic probe inspected the complete output immediately after
the full 1,404-task ioctl returned `-110`. The output and input sentinels were
unchanged:

  changed=0 input-changed=0 completed=False wrote-output=False

This result shows no externally visible progress. It does not identify the
internal task where execution stopped.

Ten-second persistence result
-----------------------------

An exact original-layout one-task probe on queue 1 still timed out after the
KMD poll increased from one second to ten seconds. The task manager stayed
non-idle and reported no hardware error:

  status=0x0 committed=0x400000
  errors=0x22222222/0x22222222/0x2222

The total probe wall time was 12.05 seconds. The timeout is a persistent hang,
not a slow first task.

The timeout diagnostic also read two task-manager interrupt lines:

  irq0 count=0x2 info=0x5400000 unk1=0x0 tmst=0x38402da7 unk2=0x1 ack=0x2
  irq1 count=0x1 info=0x5400000 unk1=0x0 tmst=0x38402da7 unk2=0x1

Default-queue control
---------------------

A clean boot loaded a nonintrusive KMD that reads each interrupt event once
inside the normal task-manager handler. A known-good one-task graph completed
on the default queue:

  changed=393216 input-changed=0
  finite=True completed=True wrote-output=True
  PRODUCTION_ANEC_EXECUTION_OK

The exact Qwen one-task graph then timed out on the same default queue:

  task-layout=original qid=default
  TimeoutError: [Errno 110] Connection timed out

The handler found zero queued interrupt events after both submissions. The
earlier queue-1 interrupt tuple therefore does not identify the timeout cause.
Queue selection is also not the cause: the known-good control completed on
queue 4, while Qwen timed out on queues 1 and 4.

Next experiment
---------------

Replace only Qwen task zero's ten-word task envelope with the known-good task
zero envelope. Keep Qwen's register body, linked layout, kernel, and buffers.
This isolates the descriptor protocol from the compiled compute payload.
Exact-graph isolation correction
--------------------------------

The exact regenerated HWX content matches the deployed ANEC content.
The exact 32-input padded replay still reaches the same task-20 failure.
A one-layer graph also reaches task 20 and does not complete.
A QKV-only graph commits its early chain but does not return to idle.
A two-task 2048-wide linear graph commits its terminal task but does not return to idle.

The linear graph uses the same real Qwen in_proj_qkv weight on both systems.
Its HWX task stream is 0x474 bytes, so the aligned kernel boundary is 0x480.
The converter and KMD both use that 0x480 boundary.
Exact tensor packing and kernel placement do not explain the linear failure.

TM_COMMITTED completion experiment
----------------------------------

A diagnostic KMD accepted a stable terminal committed count as success.
It logged:

  tm completion=terminal status=0x0 committed=0x400001 td_count=2
  submit: execute returned 0

Userspace did not observe an output change.
The bounded probe exited through its external timeout and wrote no output file.
The known-good control still completed through TM_STATUS idle.

Conclusion: TM_COMMITTED records accepted descriptors, not completed compute.
The terminal-count fallback is unsafe and remains diagnostic-only.
A valid result requires idle status, a returned submit, and a verified output change.

Exact macOS linear control
--------------------------

The exact two-task linear graph executes on macOS ANE through ANEForge.
Observed output:

  shape=(1, 2048) finite=True nonzero=2040 min=-0.150635 max=0.128418
  head=0.010345,-0.012589,-0.006790,-0.055878,0.044006,0.017349,0.006866,-0.001404

This control proves the graph, real weight, and input can execute on the M1 ANE family.
It does not prove that direct ANECCompile output equals the e5rt prepared program.

Compiler-generation boundary
----------------------------

The direct compiler was run against the same captured linear MIL and weights.
Observed target results:

  ARCH=h11 RC=1 SIZE=0 callback_status=1
  ARCH=h12 RC=1 SIZE=0 callback_status=1
  ARCH=h13 RC=0 SIZE=8421376 callback_status=0

All available compiler hosts run macOS 26.6.1 or 26.6.2.
No available host retains the older compiler generation.
Target selection cannot reproduce the older self-contained descriptor format.

Program-lifecycle boundary
--------------------------

The current Linux driver fork is commit 1e0afd832cf171be543d18069cef726aae2b9634.
Its task path maps BARs, sets task size, address, and NID, then rings the doorbell.
It exposes no ANE program-create or program-prepare operation.

The macOS ANEServices export table exposes separate operations:

  ANE_ProgramCreate
  ANE_ProgramPrepare
  ANE_ProgramSendRequest

The leading missing contract is modern program preparation before task submission.
This is consistent with old-format controls executing and all fresh modern graphs stopping.
Further input rewrites, completion shortcuts, and isolated descriptor-bit sweeps are closed.

Capture-surface probes
----------------------

A bounded scanner ran immediately before and after e5rt encode.
The exact linear graph still executed successfully.
The scanner found zero copies of the raw modern task signature in readable process memory.

User-process IOKit interposition did not expose a stable submit payload.
The first path observed only high-frequency status-style calls.
The async and struct paths destabilized execution before a valid output.

The ANEServices methods are visible in the dyld export table.
An injected library could not bind those C++ symbols through normal interposition.
A pending LLDB breakpoint on ANE_ProgramPrepare also did not resolve during e5rt startup.
The bounded debug run timed out and was terminated.
A plain post-debug run still returned the exact verified linear output.
The prepared program is therefore outside the current user-process capture surface.

Next experiment
---------------

Capture one compiler-consistent prepared modern program from macOS.
If capture remains unavailable, implement the complete program-create and prepare lifecycle.
Prove that work first with the two-task linear graph before QKV or full Qwen runs.
